From 1602a3095b12f20b584066ac88c37a9b2ad07f1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Han <danielhanchen@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 18:27:29 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] ggml-cuda: optional cuDNN attention for unmasked
 FLASH_ATTN_EXT and SAGE_ATTN

GGML_CUDA_CUDNN=ON (default OFF, Linux) builds a cuDNN fused-attention path
through the header-only cudnn-frontend v1.26.0 (MIT; fetched by CMake or taken
from GGML_CUDA_CUDNN_FRONTEND_DIR) with NV_CUDNN_FRONTEND_USE_DYNAMIC_LOADING:
only cudnn.h is needed at build time and libcudnn.so.9 is dlopen'ed on first use
(GGML_CUDA_CUDNN_LIB overrides the name), so the binary has no cuDNN link
dependency and runs unchanged where cuDNN is missing.

On NVIDIA sm80+ it can take unmasked, sink/ALiBi/softcap-free attention with equal
Q/K/V head sizes (multiple of 8, up to 256) and at least 2^20 attention scores,
GQA and batch included, for FLASH_ATTN_EXT and for SAGE_ATTN in AUTO mode (an
explicitly requested Sage variant keeps its kernel). Q (F32) is narrowed to F16
into dst's own bytes, F16 K/V are read in place with their strides (F32 K/V are
narrowed), cuDNN writes F16 O to scratch after dst and one kernel widens it into
dst. The scratch is part of the op's graph allocation (max with the existing
kernels' needs). Plans are built once per shape and cached; a shape cuDNN
declines, a failed build or execute, stream capture, Turing and older, or a
missing library keep the existing kernels.

By default FLASH_ATTN_EXT takes it on sm89 and sm100 and SAGE_ATTN on sm100,
where it measured faster; the existing kernels were faster on sm86 (both ops)
and sm89 (Sage), and other architectures were not measured.
GGML_CUDA_CUDNN_ATTN=0|1 and GGML_CUDA_CUDNN_SAGE=0|1 turn it off or on for every
sm80+ GPU, GGML_CUDA_CUDNN_ATTN_BF16=1 runs BF16, GGML_CUDA_CUDNN_ATTN_LOG=1
logs each call.

test-backend-ops: unmasked cases at the MiniMax-H3 head layout (odd and aligned
lengths, GQA with batch, F32 K/V, permuted Q) and unmasked perf cases.
---
